Here is a LaTeX abstract suitable for a submission to a nuclear physics journal (such as *Physical Review C* or *European Physical Journal A*):

```latex
\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The structural features and cluster configurations of light stable and radioactive nuclei---specifically $^{9}\text{Be}$, $^{14}\text{N}$, and $^{8}\text{B}$---were investigated using the peripheral dissociation mechanism at energies exceeding $1\,\text{A GeV}$. Stacks of Nuclear Track Emulsions (NTE) exposed to relativistic ion beams at the JINR Nuclotron provided full three-dimensional spatial reconstruction of fragment topologies with sub-micron angular resolution. By selecting extremely peripheral nuclear interactions characterized by minimal momentum transfer, the fundamental cluster degrees of freedom of the projectile nuclei were probed in a "cold" fragmentation regime. 

For the proton-halo candidate $^{8}\text{B}$, primary dissociation modes including $^{7}\text{Be} + p$ and $2\alpha + p$ were isolated, confirming the strong spatial decoupling of the valence proton. Studies of $^{9}\text{Be}$ dissociation revealed a clear dominance of the $2\alpha + n$ channel, driven predominantly by the decay of the unstable $^{8}\text{Be}$ ground state. In the fragmentation of $^{14}\text{N}$, multi-body final states involving single and multiple $\alpha$-particles (including $3\alpha$ ensemble channels) were categorized and quantified. Invariant mass distributions of the relativistic fragment ensembles were reconstructed using high-precision angular measurements. These results highlight the continued efficacy of the NTE technique for mapping complex multi-body cluster states and halo phenomena in light exotic nuclei at relativistic energies.
\end{abstract}

\end{document}
```

### Key Highlights Included in the Abstract:
* **Physics Concepts:** Alpha-clustering, proton-halo structure, peripheral dissociation, relativistic kinematics, invariant mass reconstruction.
* **Nuclei Covered:** $^9\text{Be}$, $^{14}\text{N}$, and $^8\text{B}$.
* **Experimental Method:** Nuclear Track Emulsion (NTE) technique, sub-micron spatial resolution, $3\text{D}$ track measurement.
* **Energy Regime:** $>1\text{ A GeV}$ (referencing the JINR Nuclotron context where much of this specific research is traditionally conducted).
